## CLOSED — the gate above killed it. Design kept, not open work (§5.122/§5.123)
**The syntax guard's stranded-tail branch: 0 successes in 181 attempts.**
`locode/tools/fs.py:884` — when an edit would introduce a SyntaxError the
message forks:
  * error INSIDE the text the model supplied  -> n= 22, **55%** next edit lands
  * error OUTSIDE it ("leftover tail")        -> n=181, **0%**, 52% fail again

THE MECHANISM (measured, §5.123). Three of my own hypotheses died first: the
message DOES render a line-numbered snippet; `new` is balanced in 79%; the prose
diagnosis is CORRECT. What is true: **all 217 come from `replace_lines`, never
`edit_file`.** replace_lines takes path/start/end/new — a BLIND line range, the
model never quotes what it replaces — so it cannot see its range clips a
docstring. 89% are `unterminated triple-quoted string literal` reported a median
**46 lines** downstream; 69% of ALL b127+ replace_lines failures are this shape.
The message is not wrong, it is UNACTIONABLE: "extend the region to cover that
whole block" never names the line the block starts at. And `_seam_window`
(fs.py:842) spans min(hi,err)-2..err+2, so its intended 2 lines of context
render a median **34** (124 of 245 long enough to be clipped).

THE DESIGN (pre-registered, not built):
 1. Two bounded ±3 windows — seam and error — never the gap between them.
 2. Compute the clipped construct's real boundary (we hold start/end and a
    before-text that parses, so the split delimiter is derivable).
 3. Deliver as a ONE-FIELD change in the 87%-recovery ambiguous-match shape:
    "your range 16-30 splits the docstring spanning 12-39 — send the SAME call
    with `start: 12`". Not "re-read the file and extend the region".
 4. Lever 0f: none of this goes in the tool description.

